From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 01:29:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: record sequential encoders into one Metal
 command buffer

Every render pass and every run of compute or copy work outside a render
pass began a Metal command buffer of its own, and the recording kept all
of them until its submission completed. A Metal 4 command buffer's first
render encoder takes about 2.5 MiB of command allocator storage and its
first compute encoder about 0.7 MiB, while each further encoder in the
same command buffer takes about 0.28 MiB. Zink ends its render pass for
every texture upload, so a burst of glyph uploads alternates copies and
render passes: a Plasma desktop's first frame recorded 904 command
buffers into one submission, about 1.5 GiB of command storage at those
costs, of which the footprint charges the pages the encoders wrote. On an
iPad the app's footprint rose from 1.78 to 2.81 GiB within a second of that
frame, and the system killed it.

Render encoders, and compute encoders opened while no render encoder is
open, now share one main command buffer on the gfx allocator. The 904
encoders of that recording become one command buffer. Compute that must
run before or after a whole render pass keeps a command buffer of its
own. It is appended before the main one, which is sound only while the
render encoder is the main one's first. When it is not, the pass is split
there, and a pass that needed such compute begins its next encoder in a
new main command buffer. Post-render compute ends the main command buffer
with the pass.

A split during a draw flushes the draw's state into the resumed encoder.
A split anywhere else leaves that to the next draw, because the state need
not be complete yet: flushing it at a transform feedback begin, before a
dynamic vertex input was set, made Metal abort creating the render
encoder.

KK_WORKAROUND_18 depended on a restarted render encoder starting a new
command buffer: a second render encoder in the same command buffer drops
another layer's primitives exactly as a second draw does. Every render
encoder of a layered or multiview pass therefore starts a command buffer.

Even then, Metal loses leading 1984-vertex batches of a layer-selecting
draw whose command buffer starts while the previous encoder's is still
running and is ordered behind it only by a barrier: a 16384-point second
layer after a 1048576-point first one lost points in 20 of 20 fresh
processes, and a standalone Metal 4 program without Mesa loses them the
same way. Letting the earlier command buffer complete first avoids it, so
a command buffer that begins a layered or multiview encoder now goes to
Metal in a commit of its own, behind a queue-level signal of, and a wait
for, the queue's own shared event. The signal is recorded with the
queue's Vulkan signals, so a killed submission cannot leave the wait
holding the queue. Each such encoder costs a Metal commit and a queue
wait, about 10 to 13 microseconds, or 2.5 to 3.5 percent of a pass whose
every draw switches layers. kosmickrisp_layered_draw_sequence now draws
16384 points into its second layer, which catches that loss in nearly
every fresh process.

A pool may now be destroyed with a command buffer still recording without
leaking its open command buffers.
kosmickrisp_query_pool_lifetime throws such a recording away, and resets,
begins or frees one, after destroying the timestamp query pool it wrote to:
closing its encoders must not reach the pool's released Metal objects.
