+The restart alone does not remove the loss. A restarted encoder whose command
+buffer starts while the one before it is still running occasionally loses
+whole 1984-vertex batches from the start of its layer-selecting draw, and a
+standalone Metal 4 program without Mesa loses them the same way. The loss
+needs an encoder that draws into a different layer than the encoder before it,
+is ordered after that encoder only by a barrier or a fence, and follows one
+that drew many primitives without clearing the attachment: a 16384-point draw
+after a 1048576-point one loses points in nearly every fresh process, and a
+second 1048576-point draw in one to five in a hundred. Load ops, barrier forms,
+fences, extra encoders, private storage, a hazard-tracked attachment outside a
+heap and residency changes all leave it, while letting the earlier command
+buffer complete first removes it, whether by a CPU wait, a queue-level event
+wait or a second command queue.
+
+A command buffer that begins a layered or multiview pass's encoder is
+therefore committed on its own: the queue first signals a shared event of its
+own and waits for that value, so the command buffer starts only once
+everything committed before it has completed. The signal is recorded with the
+queue's Vulkan signals, so a submission Metal kills cannot leave the wait
+holding the queue. The cost is a Metal commit and a queue wait per such
+encoder, about 10 to 13 microseconds each on an Apple silicon Mac, or 2.5 to
+3.5 percent of a pass whose every draw switches layers; other passes are
+untouched. ``kosmickrisp_layered_draw_sequence`` draws only 16384 points into
+its second layer, which loses points in nearly every fresh process without the
+wait.
+
 Restarting an encoder mid-pass has to preserve everything the open encoder
 was carrying. Vertex amplification was the one piece that did not survive:
 ``vkCmdClearAttachments`` turns amplification off because the meta clear
@@ -162,6 +193,8 @@ encoder start, and ``kosmickrisp_multiview_clear_attachments`` covers it.
 
